    en: `
<strong>Lesotho's culture is closely connected with the Basotho people</strong>, who make up the majority of the country's population. National identity is strongly shaped by the Sesotho language, traditional ways of life, the history of the kingdom, and the legacy of <strong>Moshoeshoe I</strong>, who united the Basotho in the 19th century. Despite modernization and strong influences from South Africa, traditional customs remain visible in everyday life, particularly in rural and highland communities.

One of the most recognizable symbols of Lesotho's culture is the <strong>Basotho blanket</strong>. These woollen blankets are used not only for protection from the country's cold mountain climate but also as an important part of traditional clothing. They are worn over ordinary clothes, and their distinctive patterns and ways of wearing them can carry cultural meaning. Another famous symbol is the <strong>Basotho pony</strong>, which has been used for generations as a means of transport across difficult mountain terrain. Horses remain an important part of highland culture and are particularly visible during traditional events.

<strong>Music and dance</strong> play an important role in Basotho cultural life. Traditional songs accompany public gatherings, celebrations, and ceremonies, while music is also used to tell stories and preserve collective memory. One of the best-known traditional instruments is the <strong>setolo-tolo</strong>, a mouth-blown instrument traditionally played by men. Modern music in Lesotho combines traditional influences with South African and international musical styles.

<strong>Traditional clothing and crafts</strong> remain an important part of the country's cultural heritage. In addition to Basotho blankets, local crafts include woven products, baskets, jewellery, and woollen goods. The traditional conical straw hat known as the <strong>mokorotlo</strong> is another important national symbol and is even represented on the flag of Lesotho. Its distinctive shape is associated with Basotho culture and the mountainous environment of the country.

<strong>Religious life in Lesotho is predominantly Christian</strong>. Christianity became widely established during the 19th century through missionary activity, particularly during the period when the Basotho kingdom was taking shape. Today, most of the population identifies with various Christian denominations, with Roman Catholicism and Protestant churches being particularly prominent. The Roman Catholic Church has played an important role, as have several Protestant denominations that developed from early missionary movements.

At the same time, Christianity did not completely replace <strong>traditional Basotho beliefs</strong>. Respect for ancestors, the importance of elders, and certain traditional ceremonies continue to exist alongside Christian practices. Basotho culture places strong emphasis on family, community, hospitality, and respect for tradition. Many people therefore maintain elements of traditional cultural heritage while also practising Christianity.

<strong>Language is one of the most important elements of national culture</strong>. Sesotho is the main language of everyday communication and one of the country's two official languages, alongside English. It is used in families, education, music, literature, and public life. English is widely used in government, business, and higher education. The combination of a traditional African language and English reflects Lesotho's history and its close connections with the wider southern African region.

Lesotho's cultural heritage is particularly visible in <strong>traditional celebrations, ceremonies, and community life in the highlands</strong>. The history of the kingdom, the legacy of Moshoeshoe I, national symbols, music, clothing, horsemanship, and language create a distinctive cultural identity that differs noticeably from those of neighbouring countries. The combination of Christian traditions, ancient Basotho heritage, and modern southern African influences makes Lesotho one of the region's most distinctive cultures.
`,
